[R0477] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of Science | type=Conference paper
TITLE: ChatSOC: A Large Language Model Powered Autonomous Agent for Security Operations Center
KEYWORDS: Agents; High energy physics; Network security; Security systems; Automated response; Cyber-attacks; High-energy physics; Language model; Operational tasks; Predefined sets; Security operation center; Security operations; Security tools; Standardized procedure; Autonomous agents
ABSTRACT: To defend against numerous cyberattacks, the Security Operations Center at the Institute of High Energy Physics (IHEP SOC) was established in 2021. IHEP SOC implemented a standardized procedure for invoking several security tools to execute automated response tasks. This standardized process utilizes a predefined set of tools to ensure that the IHEP SOC maintains minute-level response times, with a remarkably low false block rate of one in ten thousand. However, the fixed process of tool invoking is often challenging to update and manage, and it typically addresses isolated security operational tasks. To address this issue, this paper introduces ChatSOC, an autonomous agent for network security operations empowered by an LLM, capable of completing various operational tasks: identification, protection, detection, and response. ChatSOC streamlines these operations by dynamically planning and executing tasks without relying on fixed processes for tool invocation. Through its deployment at the Institute of High Energy Physics (IHEP), ChatSOC has proven highly effective, enhancing security operations by automatically planning and invoking different tools. © Copyright owned by the author(s)

[R0478] year=2026 | venue=CANDO-EPE 2026 - IEEE 8th International Conference and Workshop in Obuda on Electrical and Power Engineering, Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Data validation: SOC and LLM approaches
KEYWORDS: Data Validation; Large Language Models; Retrieval-Augmented Generation; Security Operation Center; Autonomous agents; Computation theory; Computational methods; Cost benefit analysis; Multi agent systems; Reliability analysis; Security systems; Semantics; Verification; Adversarial environments; Data validation; Driven system; High volumes; Language model; Large language model; Paper analysis; Pre-processing step; Retrieval-augmented generation; Security operation center; Economic and social effects
ABSTRACT: Data validation has evolved from a static preprocessing step into a continuous requirement in modern AI-driven systems operating in high-volume and adversarial environments. This paper analyzes validation challenges across two domains: Security Operations Centers (SOC) and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines. While traditional validation focuses on structural correctness and consistency, SOC and RAG systems require additional guarantees related to semantic integrity, temporal validity, and resilience against adversarial manipulation.Based on a structured analysis of recent approaches, we identify common validation patterns across these domains and highlight the increasing role of AI-Assisted techniques, LLM-based reasoning, multi-Agent validation, and reliability-Aware retrieval. To support this analysis, we provide a comparative overview of existing validation methods, emphasizing their trade-offs in terms of real-Time applicability and computational cost.Building on these observations, we outline a conceptual, agent-based validation architecture that integrates structural, semantic, and adversarial validation layers. A feasibility-oriented analysis shows that such systems are most practical as selective and hybrid pipelines, where high-cost LLM-based validation is applied only to high-risk cases. We conclude that data validation should be treated as a multi-layered architectural component essential for ensuring trust and robustness in both AI and security systems. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0479] year=2025 | venue=IEEE Intelligent Systems | type=Article
TITLE: Leveraging Large Language Models for Autonomous Cyber Defense: Insights from CAGE-2 Simulations
KEYWORDS: Cyber-defense; Human like; Language model; Model agents; Model temperatures; Network observation; New applications; Performance; Reinforcement learning agent; Simulated networks; Reinforcement learning
ABSTRACT: The ability of large language models (LLMs) to interpret and generate human-like text, along with their inbuilt knowledge and reasoning skills, has opened the door for many new applications. We develop a novel LLM agent as an autonomous cyber defender and benchmark it using the CAGE-2 public challenge, which evaluates submitted agents in a simulated network. The agent uses a pretrained LLM to interpret a text description of the challenge and choose the best defensive action given a network observation. Its performance is on par with the most successful reinforcement learning agent submitted to CAGE-2 with the advantages of explainability and avoiding training episodes, but with the cost of requiring LLM prompt engineering. Finally, we test the LLM agent experimentally to explore how LLM temperature and prompt complexity affect performance. We show that higher complexity prompts significantly improve performance, while LLM temperature has only a minor effect. © 2001-2011 IEEE.

[R0480] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of the ACM Conference on AI and Agentic Systems, CAIS 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: FORGE: Self-Evolving Agent Memory with No Weight Updates via Population Broadcast
KEYWORDS: cyber defense; LLM agents; memory evolution; POMDP; population-based training; prompt-only learning; self-improvement; Artificial intelligence; Computer software reusability; Decision making; Failure (mechanical); Failure analysis; Stochastic models; Cyber-defense; Decisions makings; LLM agent; Memory evolution; Natural languages; POMDP; Population-based training; Prompt-only learning; Self-improvement; Weight update; Stochastic systems
ABSTRACT: Can LLM agents improve decision-making through self-generated memory without gradient updates? We propose FORGE (Failure-Optimized Reflective Graduation and Evolution), a staged, population-based protocol that evolves prompt-injected natural-language memory for hierarchical ReAct agents. FORGE wraps a Reflexion-style inner loop, where a dedicated reflection agent (using the same underlying LLM, no distillation from a stronger model) converts failed trajectories into reusable knowledge artifacts: textual heuristics (Rules), few-shot demonstrations (Examples), or both (Mixed), with an outer loop that propagates the best-performing instance's memory to the population between stages and freezes converged instances via a graduation criterion. We evaluate on CybORG CAGE-2, a stochastic network-defense POMDP at a 30-step horizon against the B_line attacker, where all four tested LLM families (Gemini-2.5-Flash-Lite, Grok-4-Fast, Llama-4-Maverick, Qwen3-235B) exhibit strongly negative, heavy-tailed zero-shot rewards. Compared against both a zero-shot baseline and a Reflexion baseline (isolated single-stream learning), FORGE improves average evaluation return by 1.7-7.7 × over zero-shot and by 29-72% over Reflexion in all 12 model-representation conditions, reducing major-failure rates (below - 100) to as low as ∼1%. We find that (1) population broadcast is the critical mechanism, with a no-graduation ablation confirming that broadcast carries the performance gains while graduation primarily saves compute; (2) Examples achieves the strongest returns for three of four models, while Rules offers the best cost-reliability profile with ∼40% fewer tokens; and (3) weaker baseline models benefit disproportionately, suggesting FORGE may mitigate capability gaps rather than amplify strong models. All evidence is confined to CAGE-2 B_line; cross-family findings are directional evidence. © 2026 Copyright is held by the owner/author(s). Publication rights licensed to ACM.

[R0481] year=2025 | venue=International Conference on Evaluation of Novel Approaches to Software Engineering, ENASE - Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: CyberGuardian 2: Integrating LLMs and Agentic AI Assistants for Securing Distributed Networks
