ABSTRACT: Robust cybersecurity measures are essential to protect complex information systems from a variety of cyber threats, which requires sophisticated security solutions. This paper explores the integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) to improve cybersecurity operations within Security Operations Centers (SOCs). The proposed framework has a modular plugin architecture where Agentic AI controls the information flow, includes Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG), protection methods for human-chatbot interactions and tools for managing tasks such as database interactions, code generation and execution. By utilizing these techniques, the framework aims to streamline the workflows of SOC analysts, allowing them to focus on critical tasks rather than redundant activities. The study also explores the dynamic customization of LLMs based on client data, user experience, potential risks and language preferences to ensure a user-centric approach. The results show improvements in efficiency and effectiveness and highlight the potential of LLMs in cybersecurity applications. Copyright © 2025 by SCITEPRESS - Science and Technology Publications, Lda.

[R0482] year=2026 | venue=Procedia Computer Science | type=Conference paper
TITLE: LLM-Generated Heuristics for Guiding Reinforcement Learning in Autonomous Cyber Defense
KEYWORDS: Autonomous Cyber Defense; Large Language Models; Reinforcement Learning; Teacher-Guided Reinforcement Learning; Computational methods; Computer aided instruction; Intelligent agents; Network security; Personnel training; Teaching; Autonomous cybe defense; Cyber-defense; Language model; Large language model; Performance; Reinforcement learning agent; Reinforcement learnings; Teacher-guided reinforcement learning; Teachers'; Reinforcement learning
ABSTRACT: Reinforcement learning has been successfully applied to autonomous cyber defense, but it suffers from high sample complexity which hinders its adaptability to new environments and threats. LLM-based teacher-guided techniques have been used to mitigate these issues, but LLMs have a high inference cost and use a great deal of computational resources, making them difficult to apply in real-world autonomous cyber defense scenarios. In this paper, we introduce a novel LLM-based action masking strategy for autonomous cyber defense where an LLM produces a collection of heuristics to guide reinforcement learning. The LLM generated heuristics are implemented as action masks throughout the training process as a means of providing guidance to the RL agent in order to mitigate poor initial performance. We distinguish between recommendation heuristics, which specify actions that should be taken in a specific scenario, and constraint heuristics, which specify actions that should not be taken in a specific scenario. We apply action masks according to these heuristics in two ways: 1) only allowing recommended actions to be selected, and 2) disallowing actions based on constraint heuristics. We compare our LLM enhanced reinforcement learning agent to a baseline reinforcement learning agent with no LLM guidance in the CybORG++ reinforcement learning environment. We evaluate training convergence and performance across 10 independent runs and analyze how the heuristic-based action masks influence action selection. © 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V.

[R0483] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Smart Applications, Communications and Networking, SmartNets 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Can LLM Agents Replace Reinforcement Learning Agents in Cyber Defence Automation: A Case Study Using the DARPA CAGE-2 Challenge
