TITLE: BRETAGNE: Building a Reproducible and Efficient Training AI Gym for Network Environments
KEYWORDS: Autonomous Cyber Defence; Cyber Security; Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning; Network Virtualization; Autonomous agents; Chatbots; Computer viruses; Cyber attacks; HTTP; Intelligent agents; Reinforcement learning; Virtual environments; Virtualization; Autonomous cybe defense; Cyber security; Cyber-defense; Multi-agent reinforcement learning; Network environments; Network simulation; Network virtualization; Real-world networks; Simulation environment; Virtualization technologies; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: This paper introduces BRETAGNE, a network simulation environment designed to serve as a training ground for autonomous defense agents using hybrid AI models in simulations. The platform integrates docker, a lightweight virtualization technology orchestrated by Kathara with widely used network protocols such as BGP, OSPF, HTTP, SSH and others to simulate production-like environments, thereby addressing the limitations of existing simulators that often lack applicability in real-world networks. We present a multi-agent architecture involving blue, red, green, and white agents, designed to create dynamic, communicative environments for training purposes. Furthermore, our work includes a comparative analysis of large language models (LLMs) for detecting cyber attacks, which highlights the benefits and constraints of using AI for autonomous decision-making in cybersecurity. Overall, the BRETAGNE framework offers a realistic and scalable solution for the training and deployment of autonomous agents in operational networks. © 2024 IEEE.

[R0494] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - 2025 IEEE Conference on Artificial Intelligence, CAI 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: SkyQuery: Evaluating LLMs for Decision Making in the CAGE Challenge
KEYWORDS: cage; deception; llm; Network security; Cage; Cyber-defense; Deception; Decision-making process; Decisions makings; Defence systems; Llm; Simulation environment; Decision making
ABSTRACT: ─This paper presents a novel framework for developing autonomous cyber defense agents that incorporate defender goals into their decision-making processes. Evaluated within the CAGE Challenge 2 simulation environment, the framework enables agents to adapt their strategies to align with specific objectives, such as maximizing rewards or deploying deception. Leveraging previously validated strategies, the proposed approach enhances the adaptability and effectiveness of cyber defense systems, providing a foundation for addressing evolving threats in dynamic cyber environments. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0495] year=2026 | venue=Applied Sciences (Switzerland) | type=Article
TITLE: Design of a Security Framework for Multi-Agent Systems Based on Model Context Protocol in SOC Environments
KEYWORDS: agentic AI security; MCP security; model context protocol; multi-agent systems; prompt injection; security operations center; SIEM integration; tool poisoning; Access control models; Autonomous agents; Intelligent agents; Middleware; Network security; Pipelines; Programmable logic controllers; Security systems; System-on-chip; Agentic AI security; Model context protocol; Model context protocol security; Model contexts; Multiagent systems (MASs); Prompt injection; Protocol security; Security operation center; SIEM integration; Tool poisoning; algorithm; Article; content analysis; environment; knowledge; large language model; logging; memory; methodology; model context protocol; performance; post hoc analysis; protocol; responsibility; secure hash algorithm; security; security operations center environment; validation study; vulnerability; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Featured Application: The proposed framework can be deployed as a drop-in middleware layer to secure MCP-based AI agent pipelines in production Security Operations Centers, protecting SIEM-integrated multi-agent deployments against attacks such as tool poisoning, prompt injection, and confused deputy exploitation without modifying the underlying agents or MCP servers. Security Operations Centers (SOCs) rely on Level 1 analysts to triage increasing alert volumes amid alert fatigue and tool fragmentation. LLM-based multi-agent systems using the Model Context Protocol (MCP) are being adopted to automate these tasks, but their autonomy and tool access expose them to attacks such as tool poisoning, indirect prompt injection, and confused deputy exploitation. To address this gap, this work proposes a security framework for MCP-based multi-agent SOC pipelines, implemented as a middleware layer comprising a tool registration validator and five execution layers: access control, rate limiting, input validation, output validation, and audit logging. The framework is applied to a triage-enrichment-response pipeline connected to a Wazuh SIEM through a custom MCP server. Of the 35 attack vectors considered in a threat model derived from different threat taxonomies, including OWASP, MITRE ATLAS, and ATFAA, 29 are addressable at the middleware level and are covered by the framework’s controls. These controls are then validated experimentally using a purpose-built malicious MCP server and targeted test-harness injections, organized into six test suites that together exercise the covered vectors across 600 executions. Every attack instance in the evaluated threat model was blocked, none bypassed the framework, and no legitimate call in the evaluated set was incorrectly rejected; obfuscated variants, however, evade the lexical content-inspection controls, delimiting the scope of this result. A full-pipeline demonstration confirms that the framework preserves benign operational outputs. These results indicate that systematic middleware controls can secure MCP-based agentic SOC deployments without modifying the underlying agents or MCP servers. © 2026 by the authors.

[R0496] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - 46th IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy Workshops, SPW 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Exploring Traffic Simulation and Cybersecurity Strategies Using Large Language Models
KEYWORDS: Autonomous Connected Vehicles; Cybersecurity; Large Language Model (LLM); Multi-Agent LLM; Simulation; Accident prevention; Cybersecurity; Intelligent agents; Intelligent systems; Intelligent vehicle highway systems; Motor transportation; Network security; Street traffic control; Travel time; Autonomous connected vehicle; Cyber security; Cyber-attacks; Language model; Large language model; Multi agent; Multi-agent large language model; Simulation; Traffic simulations; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS) are increasingly vulnerable to sophisticated cyberattacks due to their complex, interconnected nature. Ensuring the cybersecurity of these systems is paramount to maintaining road safety and minimizing traffic disruptions. This study presents a novel multi-agent framework leveraging Large Language Models (LLMs) to enhance traffic simulation and cybersecurity testing. The framework automates the creation of traffic scenarios, the design of cyberattack strategies, and the development of defense mechanisms. A case study demonstrates the framework's ability to simulate a cyberattack targeting connected vehicle broadcasts, evaluate its impact, and implement a defense mechanism that significantly mitigates traffic delays. Results show a 10.2% increase in travel time during an attack, which is reduced by 3.3% with the defense strategy. This research highlights the potential of LLM-driven multi-agent systems in advancing transportation cybersecurity and offers a scalable approach for future research in traffic simulation and cyber defense. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0497] year=2025 | venue=SITA 2025 - 15th International Conference on Intelligent Systems: Theories and Applications | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Gamification Empowered by LLM-Based Agents: A Systematic Literature Review
KEYWORDS: AI agent; Gamification; Large Language Models; LLM agent; Systematic Literature Review; Artificial intelligence; Arts computing; Behavioral research; Education computing; Intelligent agents; Reviews; Agent systems; AI agent; Gamification; Language model; Large language model; Large language model agent; Model agents; Model-based OPC; Systematic literature review; Gamification
ABSTRACT: The combination of gamification and agent systems based on Large Language Models (LLMs) presents a great opportunity in the design of adaptable, interactive systems that offer engagement and personalized interactions. In this systematic literature review, we examine the state of the art at this intersection, drawing on 11 studies published from 2019 to 2025, using the PRISMA 2020 framework, and investigating the following seven research questions related to: publication trends, areas of application, agent roles, gamification strategies, design goals, and limitations. We find LLM agents are being deployed across multiple application areas in gamified systems, in the fields of education, mental health, cyber defense, and commerce, while functioning in multiple roles such as tutors, an emotional companions, and being involved in the co-design process. Yet, we identified key challenges including weak connectivity between gamification mechanics and agents’ behaviors and capabilities, limited longitudinal evaluation, and ethical concerns for LLMs related to persuasion and autonomy of the user. Our review provides a foundation for furthering the study and development of gamification systems involving LLM agents. ©2025 IEEE.

[R0498] year=2026 | venue=Information Fusion | type=Article
TITLE: Security of LLM-based agents regarding attacks, defenses, and applications: A comprehensive survey
KEYWORDS: Attack; Defense; LLM-based agent; Security; Autonomous agents; Decision making; Human computer interaction; Network security; Security systems; Attack; Complex task; Cyber-defense; Defense; Evaluation criteria; Language model; Large language model-based agent; Model-based OPC; Reasoning engine; Security; Surveying
ABSTRACT: Large Language Model (LLM) based agents that employ an LLM as a core reasoning engine, are autonomous or semi-autonomous systems. Equipped with dedicated perception and action modules, they can sense their environment and take autonomous actions to execute complex tasks. Increasingly used for automated decision-making and real-world interactions that need multi-step planning capabilities and persistent engagement with external tools and environments, these agents face substantial and complex security risks. Compared to single-turn LLM inference, the agentic execution of complex tasks significantly expands the attack surface. Although prior work has surveyed security threats and defenses for LLM-based agents, two key issues remain to be addressed: (i) lack of a comprehensive review on attack and defense methods under unified evaluation criteria, and (ii) under-exploration of the security applications of LLM-based agents, ranging from enabling cyberattacks to strengthening cyber defense. This paper addresses these gaps with a comprehensive survey of attacks against, and defenses of, the LLM-based agents, as well as a review of the security-related applications enabled by LLM-based agents. We first introduce the foundations of LLM-based agents, and describe the structure and scope of this review. We then propose two complementary sets of evaluation criteria for rigorously evaluating the performance of attacks and defenses. Using these criteria, we analyze the strengths and limitations of the work presented in the relevant literature. In addition, we propose a taxonomy of security-related applications enabled by LLM-based agents and summarize the existing work from two perspectives: cyber offense and cyber defense. Finally, we identify key open challenges and propose future research directions to advance the secure development and deployment of LLM-based agents. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.

[R0499] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of the International Joint Conference on Autonomous Agents and Multiagent Systems, AAMAS | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Personality-Driven Decision-Making in LLM-Based Autonomous Agents
KEYWORDS: Autonomous Agents; Decision-Making; Language Agents; Large Language Models; Personality Induction; Planning; Task Selection; Behavioral research; Intelligent agents; Multi agent systems; Agent architectures; Decisions makings; Domain specific; Embeddings; Language agent; Language model; Large language model; Model-based OPC; Personality induction; Task selection; Autonomous agents; Decision making
ABSTRACT: The embedding of Large Language Models (LLMs) into autonomous agents is a rapidly developing field which enables dynamic, configurable behaviours without the need for extensive domain-specific training. In our previous work, we introduced SANDMAN, a Deceptive Agent architecture leveraging the Five-Factor OCEAN personality model, demonstrating that personality induction significantly influences agent task planning. Building on these findings, this study presents a novel method for measuring and evaluating how induced personality traits affect task selection processes-specifically planning, scheduling, and decision-making-in LLM-based agents. Our results reveal distinct task-selection patterns aligned with induced OCEAN attributes, underscoring the feasibility of designing highly plausible Deceptive Agents for proactive cyber defense strategies. © 2025 International Foundation for Autonomous Agents and Multiagent Systems (www.ifaamas.org).

[R0500] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of the 11th IEEE International Conference on Network Softwarization, NetSoft 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Towards Robust Autonomous Cyber Defence Agents Using Hybrid AI Models
KEYWORDS: Autonomous Cyber Defence; Cyber Security; Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning; Software-Defined Defence; Autonomous agents; Computation theory; Computational methods; Computer crime; Cybersecurity; Formal verification; Intelligent agents; Military applications; Military communications; Network layers; Network security; Autonomous cybe defense; Critical networks; Cyber security; Cyber-defense; Hybrid artificial intelligences; Intelligence models; Monitoring and control; Multi-agent reinforcement learning; Multi-layers; Software-defined defense; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Recent developments in Software-Defined Defence (SDD) provide the interfaces for multi-layer monitoring and control to realise Autonomous Cyber Defence (ACD) in critical network infrastructure used by the military. As a result, autonomous agents can enforce cybersecurity measures at different layers (link, IP, transport and application) using hybrid Artificial Intelligence (AI) models. For example, Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) can be combined with symbolic AI (knowledge graphs) and augmented/fine-tuned Large Language Models (LLMs) to detect, predict, protect, respond and recover from cyberattacks. This thesis starts from the hypothesis that the robustness of ACD agents can be formally verified, ensuring their safe development in mission critical environments. By applying Formal Verification (FV) techniques to MARL systems, the aim is to provide mathematically grounded guarantees such as correctness, safety, and adversarial resilience, thereby enabling trustworthy autonomous decision-making in SDD-enabled infrastructures. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0501] year=2025 | venue=2nd Asian Conference on Intelligent Technologies, ACOIT 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Cognitive Cyber Deception: An Agentic AI Framework for Dynamically Adaptive Collaborative Honeypots
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Cowrie; Cyber Deception; Cybersecurity; Generative AI; Honeypot; Large Language Models (LLM); MQTT; Vector Database; Artificial intelligence; Cognitive systems; Computation theory; Computer architecture; Computer crime; Distributed computer systems; Large datasets; Network security; Agentic AI; Cowrie; Cybe deception; Cyber security; Generative AI; Honeypots; Language model; Large language model; MQTT; Vector database; Semantics
ABSTRACT: The escalating sophistication of cyber threats necessitates a paradigm shift from static, signature-based defenses to proactive, intelligent, and adaptive security measures. Honeypots, a foundational tool in cyber deception, have traditionally been limited by their static nature, rendering them susceptible to fingerprinting and evasion by skilled adversaries. This paper presents a novel framework for a cognitive, adaptive honeypot system that leverages an agentic Generative AI framework to create a dynamic and evolving deception environment. Our system architecture integrates distributed medium-interaction Cowrie honeypots with a periodic data pipeline using the MQTT protocol, which transfers attacker activity logs to a central analysis module for deep semantic analysis. This module, whose logic is partitioned into several functional roles, interprets attacker intent, identifies complex Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs), and autonomously generates tailored bash scripts to dynamically reconfigure the honeypot's environment, thereby enhancing its realism and prolonging attacker engagement. A Pinecone vector database provides the system with long-term contextual memory, enabling the recognition of recurring attack patterns and facilitating more sophisticated, stateful deception strategies. Comparative analysis demonstrates that our adaptive honeypot detains attackers over 400% longer than a static counterpart, captures a significantly richer dataset of malicious activities, and successfully elicits more advanced attack tools and payloads. This research pioneers a closed-loop, AI-driven deception-feedback mechanism, laying the groundwork for next-generation autonomous cyber defense systems. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0502] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - IEEE Military Communications Conference MILCOM | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Explaining Hybrid RL Agents for Cyber Defense using SHAP and Reward Decomposition
KEYWORDS: Autonomous Cyber Defense; Deep Reinforcement Learning; Explainable Artificial Intelligence; Autonomous agents; Computation theory; Computational methods; Decision making; Deep learning; Intelligent agents; Multi agent systems; Network security; Autonomous cybe defense; Cyber security; Cyber-defense; Explainable artificial intelligence; Hybrid architectures; Language model; Learning rules; Military networks; Reinforcement learnings; Shapley; Formal verification
ABSTRACT: In high-stakes domains such as cybersecurity for military networks, the ability to interpret and trust the behavior of autonomous agents is essential. In this work, we apply post hoc explainability methods to a Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL)-based cyber defense agent developed for the CAGE Challenge 2. The agent employs a hybrid architecture that integrates DRL, rule-based systems, and Large Language Models (LLMs). We analyze the agent's decision-making using two complementary explainability techniques: SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) and reward decomposition. SHAP highlights which input features most influence the agent's actions, while reward decomposition attributes the agent's rewards to semantically meaningful environmental events. Our results show that the agent prioritizes clean, low-risk host states and exhibits a conservative strategy shaped by penalties associated with administrator access and restore actions. Furthermore, we propose possible enhancements such as LLM-generated textual explanations and formal verification to improve trust, transparency, and usability. This work lays the foundation for extending explainability methods to multi-agent reinforcement learning settings, with a focus on future Software-Defined Defense (SDD) scenarios. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0503] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE 5th International Conference on AI in Cybersecurity, ICAIC 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: The Evolution of Agentic AI in Cybersecurity: From Single LLM Reasoners to Multi-Agent Systems and Autonomous Pipelines
KEYWORDS: agentic AI; AI safety and verification; benchmarking of AI agents; cybersecurity automation; large language models; multi-agent systems; security operations center (SOC); Automation; Autonomous agents; Benchmarking; Cybersecurity; Decision making; Intelligent agents; Network security; Pipelines; Security systems; Agentic AI; AI safety and verification; Benchmarking of AI agent; Cyber security; Cybersecurity automation; Language model; Large language model; Multiagent systems (MASs); Security operation center; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Cybersecurity operations are increasingly adopting agentic AI solutions due to the time-critical and complex decision-making in security operations centers (SOCs). While large language models (LLMs) are good with summarization tasks or interpreting structured and unstructured reports, real-world SOC workflows have additional requirements such as access to original logs, reproducibility and accountability to triage security incidents. For example, analysts routinely correlate alerts to understand the kill-chain of the cyber-attack and analyze the event telemetries to identify the root cause event which may not have triggered an alert. Incorrect and incomplete automations in such settings can directly impact production systems and business operations.In this survey, we examine the architectural shifts from single-model assistants to tool-augmented agents, distributed multiagent systems, and schema-constrained investigation pipelines. We introduce a five-generation taxonomy that represents the evolution of agentic AI systems, their limitations and risks across different parameters, such as reasoning depth, tool interaction, memory, reproducibility and safety. We also review the emerging benchmarks to evaluate cyber-oriented agents and identify open challenges including response validation, tool-use correctness, multi-agent coordination, long-horizon reasoning and safeguards for high-impact actions. Finally, we discuss how these challenges influence deployment decisions in operational SOC environments. Our analysis provides a structured perspective on the current state of agentic AI in cybersecurity and highlights the technical and governance considerations necessary for its deployment. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0504] year=2025 | venue=2025 Cyber Research Conference - Ireland, Cyber-RCI 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: From Code to Combat: Introducing the Role of AI in Cyber and Kinetic Warfare in 2024
KEYWORDS: Artificial intelligence; autonomous weapons; cyber warfare; drone swarms; ethical AI; generative AI; hybrid warfare; military AI; security vulnerabilities; Behavioral research; Chemical warfare; Decision making; Drones; Electronic warfare; Ethical aspects; International law; Malware; Man machine systems; Military applications; Network security; Target drones; Autonomous weapon; Cyber operations; Cyber warfare; Drone swarm; Ethical artificial intelligence; Generative artificial intelligence; Hybrid warfare; Military artificial intelligence; Modern warfare; Security vulnerabilities; Artificial intelligence
ABSTRACT: Artificial intelligence (AI) has become rapidly integral to modern warfare, transforming both cyber operations and conventional military tactics in 2024. This review surveys AI deployment in offensive and defensive roles across kinetic battlefields and digital domains, examines shifting geopolitical dynamics and ethical debates, evaluates security risks and vulnerabilities, and extrapolates emerging trends. AI-enabled targeting systems, autonomous drones, and cyber defense platforms saw practical use in disputes (Ukraine, Middle East), offering unprecedented speed and efficiency while introducing vulnerabilities including adversarial attacks, algorithmic bias, and eroded accountability. Major powers accelerated AI integration, driving a global arms race. Simultaneously, urgent ethical and legal questions arose about autonomous lethal decision-making. AI tools unveiled capabilities for strengthening defenses and enabling new offensive tactics like automated malware generation, enhancing the AI attacker-defender duel. Looking ahead, generative AI, multidomain hybrid warfare, and fully autonomous systems will transform warfare while policymakers grapple with ensuring meaningful human control and international law compliance. © 2025 IEEE.


